The utility can be used to mount a file system using the ssh sftp
subprotocol,  making  a  remote directory hierarchy appear in the
local directory tree.  This functionality is  commonly  known  as
The mandatory parameters are the target host name and local mount
point.   The target host parameter can optionally contain a user‐
name whose credentials will be used by the  remote  sshd,  and  a
relative  or absolute path for the remote mount point’s root.  If
no user is given, the credentials of the user issuing  the  mount
command are used.  If no path is given, the user’s home directory
on  the  remote machine will be used.  The following command line
options are available: Opens connections  to  the  server.   Cur‐
rently,  the value has to be 1 or 2.  If 2 is specified, a second
connection is opened for the reading and writing of  data,  while
directory operations are performed on their own connection.  This
can  greatly increase directory operation performance (ls, mkdir,
etc.) if completely saturates the available  bandwidth  by  doing
bulk  data  copying.   The  default is 1.  Makes the mounted file
system NFS exportable.  If this option is used, it is very impor‐
tant to understand that can not provide complete support for  NFS
due  to the limitations in the backend.  Files are valid only for
the time that is running and in the event of e.g. a server crash,
all client retries to access files will fail.  Pass a  configura‐
tion  file to This will make it ignore the system‐wide configura‐
tion file and use instead of Converts remote to the effective gid
of the file server and vice versa.  See This flag can be used  to
give standard mount options and options to puffs.  Pass an option
to  for example For a list of valid options, see Preserve connec‐
tion.  This option makes to try to reconnect to the server if the
connection fails.  The option is very experimental and  does  not
preserve  open  files or retry current requests and should gener‐
ally only be used if the trade‐offs are well understood.   Limits
maximum  outstanding  read  requests for each node to This can be
used to improve interactive performance  on  low‐bandwidth  links
when  also  performing bulk data reads.  This flag can be used to
make the program stay on top.  The default is to detach from  the
terminal  and run in the background.  By default caches directory
contents and node attributes for 30  seconds  before  re‐fetching
from  the  server to check if anything has changed on the server.
This option is used to adjust the timeout period to  seconds.   A
value  of  0 means the cache is never valid; -1 means it is valid
indefinitely.  It is possible to force a  re‐read  regardless  of
timeout  status by sending to the process.  Note: the file system
will still free nodes when requested by the kernel and will  lose
all  cached information in doing so.  How frequently this happens
depends on system activity and the total number of available  vn‐
odes  in the system (kern.maxvnodes).  Converts remote to the ef‐
fective uid of the file server and vice versa.  This is a  simple
special  case  of the functionality of For example: you mount re‐
mote me@darkmoon as the local user "me".  If the uid of  "me"  on
the  local system is 101 and on darkmoon it is 202, you would use
to see files owned by 202 on darkmoon as owned by 101 when brows‐
ing the mount point.  Apart from the cosmetic effect, this  makes
things  like "chown me file" work.  See The following example il‐
lustrates how to mount the directory on server as user  on  local
directory with ssh transport compression enabled: mount_psshfs ‐O
Compression=yes  abc@bigiron:/usr  /mnt It is possible to use for
psshfs mounts, with SSH public key  authentication:  The  utility
first appeared in It was inspired by FUSE sshfs.  Permissions are
not  handled.  Do not expect the file system to behave except for
a single user.  Depending on if the server supports  the  statvfs
protocol  extension,  free  disk  space  may be displayed for the
mount by This information reflects the  status  at  the  server’s
mountpoint  and  may  differ  for  subdirectories under the mount
root.
